  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 258 PM CDT Sun Aug 23 2026\n\n...Synopsis...\n\nNorthwest flow prevails this afternoon into the evening, gradually \nbecoming zonal overnight. Showers and isolated thunderstorms could \nwork into parts of eastern North Dakota overnight as warm air \nadvection increases ahead of an approaching shortwave. Upper flow \nthen becomes slightly southwest late overnight into Monday morning. \nThis will be in response to the shortwave and weak upper low moving \nacross southern Manitoba late Monday into Tuesday. Showers and \nthunderstorms are expected as the associated cold front sweeps \nacross the Northern Plains into the Upper Midwest, with a chance for \nisolated severe thunderstorms Monday afternoon and evening. Warm air \nadvection ahead of the front will allow afternoon temperatures to \nclimb well into the 80s across most of the area. Slightly cooler \ntemps return on Tuesday and Wednesday. Rain chances Late Tuesday \nthrough Thursday will be minimal, with PoPs increasing once more \nlate Friday into the weekend. \n\n...Isolated Severe Thunderstorms Late Monday...\n\nSevere thunderstorm coverage on Monday afternoon and evening will \ndepend heavily on the timing of an approaching cold front. Warm air \nadvection will be strong through much of the day across eastern \nNorth Dakota and west central Minnesota; however, with remnant \nshower activity on Monday morning potentially lingering, this could \nshorten the window of potential severe weather late Monday. Looking \nat CAMs, there are several potential scenarios, ranging from \npotential splitting supercells, to elevated clusters. Model \nsoundings are consistent with nearly straight hodographs as the \nfront approaches, which continue to support splitting supercells and \nhybrid clusters. As such, a high end scenario supports supercells \ndeveloping west of the Red River during the late afternoon, with \nhazards including wind gusts to 75 mph and large hail to the size of \ngolf balls. A more likely scenario would be that we have lingering \nconvective inhibition later into the afternoon, thus delaying \nconvection until later in the evening. This would still support wind \ngusts up to 70 mph, but would transition into mainly an elevated \nhail scenario that extends into the early overnight hours. With this \nlater scenario, we could see isolated severe thunderstorms lingering \ninto Tuesday morning across parts of northwest Minnesota.",
